          <h2>1. Read-only methodology &amp; 112-table live topology</h2>
          <p>
            Following the repository-level Firestore-to-PostgreSQL cutover documented in <a href="/en/lab/meson-nosql-to-sql/">Paper 11</a>, we ran a direct <strong>read-only</strong> catalog audit against Meson's live pre-production PostgreSQL 17.11 (Supabase) backend (staging environment with zero real commercial customers). We inspected <code>pg_tables</code>, <code>pg_policies</code>, <code>pg_proc</code>, <code>pg_trigger</code>, <code>pg_index</code>, <code>pg_constraint</code>, <code>pg_publication_tables</code>, <code>extensions.pg_stat_statements</code>, official Supabase Advisor outputs (<code>get_advisors</code>), and the deployed source of all 3 active Edge Functions.
          </p>
          <p>
            The live staging database spans <strong>112 tables in <code>public</code></strong>, <strong>59 applied migrations</strong> (<code>20260512 → 20260925</code>), <strong>93 foreign-key constraints</strong> across <code>60</code> tables, <strong>238 indexes</strong> (<code>235</code> B-Tree, <code>3</code> GIN, <code>112</code> primary keys, <code>17</code> non-PK unique indexes, and <code>1</code> partial index), <strong>14 PL/pgSQL functions</strong>, <strong>12 triggers</strong>, <strong>1 view</strong> (<code>profiles</code>, verified with <code>security_invoker=true</code>), and <strong>8 Storage buckets</strong> (<code>7</code> public + private <code>tts_cache</code> capped at <code>5 MB</code>).
